        <h2>Summary / Description</h2>
        <div class="description">
          OBJECTIVES:<br>
An unavoidable consequence of many space missions is exposure to galactic cosmic radiation (GCR), which includes HZE particles like Fe, Si, and O. Estimating radiation risks to the central nervous system (CNS) is a high research priority. Investigators propose extensive analysis of how a specific region of the CNS, the hippocampus, is influenced by radiation. The hippocampus is important because it contributes to spatial learning and mood regulation, which are critical for mission success. Recently the hippocampal dentate gyrus (DG) was shown to be involved in learning challenging tasks requiring fine spatial distinction ("pattern separation"). The hippocampus is also important because it is exceptionally "neuroplastic". For example, in terms of gene transcription changes, the DG is among the most responsive CNS regions to physiological and environmental stimuli. The DG is also a site of neurogenesis throughout life, and adult neurogenesis is proposed to contribute to hippocampal function in general and pattern separation in particular. Understanding how space radiation influences DG structure and function will allow far better estimation of CNS radiation risks. Research already suggests that space radiation has a negative effect on hippocampal structure and function. However, it is unknown whether space radiation specifically influences DG functions like pattern separation. It is also unknown whether the diverse neural cells types within the DG are similarly influenced by space radiation or whether, for example, there is a cell-type specific change in translation or morphology. Investigators will use recent advances in knowledge of DG function and structure and cutting-edge but proven technical approaches to answer key remaining questions about how space radiation influences the cellular, molecular, and functional integrity of the hippocampus. 
<p>
APPROACH:<br>
In Aim 1, using behavioral tests investigators will explore their hypothesis that space radiation decreases pattern separation in a dose- and LET-dependent manner. <br>
In Aim 2, using transgenic mice investigators will use inducible YFP labeling of neural stem cells and their progeny, they will explore their hypothesis that space radiation negatively impacts early postnatally-generated DG neurons. <br>
In Aim 3, using transgenic mice developed by their colleagues that allow profiling of translating RNA in genetically-defined DG neural cells, investigators will explore their hypothesis that space radiation causes discrete changes in DG neural cell types in a dose- and LET-dependent manner. Unlike currently used approaches to assess total RNA profiles from cellularly heterogeneous samples (e.g. laser capture dissection and subsequent microarray analysis), this approach allows purification of specific cell types via ribosome tag and a quantitative comparison of translating RNA profiles in genetically-distinct DG cells.
<p>
Importantly, all mice will be 6-7 months old at time of radiation exposure in order to be approximately the age equivalent to a 30 year old human, and thus to better assess the impact of ground-based space radiation on the fully mature brain. Tissue from Aims 1-3 will be examined using immunohistochemistry, epifluorescent and confocal microscopy, stereologic cell counting, and morphological analysis, and tissue from Aim 3 will be explored using microarrays and RNAseq on the translating RNA pools and qRT-PCR.
<p>
RESULTS:<br>
This experiment is in progress. However, preliminary results are available.
<p>
Aim 1: Determine the impact of low- and high-LET radiation on hippocampal function. <br>
Building off of the second year’s work investigators have used their behavioral expertise to show that context discrimination fear conditioning (CDFC) is differentially influenced in mice that are young adult versus mature at the time of irradiation. Specifically, mice that are mature at the time of irradiation can learn more consistently and sooner to discriminate two contexts, while mice that are younger at the time of irradiation generally show decreased context separation. Investigators found this is true for both 28Si and 56Fe, with some dose- and delivery-dependency. 
<p>
Investigators also found that contextual fear conditioning (CFC) is differentially influenced by the age of the mice at the time of irradiation. For example, in general mice that are young adult at the time of irradiation show diminished CFC, while mice that are mature at the time of irradiation show no difference. Investigators found this is true for both 28Si and 56Fe, although again there is some dose- and delivery-dependency. 
<p>
Investigators found no gross difference in other behavioral and physiological measures, including weight, locomotor activity, exploration of a novel environment or dark versus light environments, and pain threshold. 
<p>
Investigators have found that hippocampal neurogenesis is decreased in most irradiated mice, regardless of the age at the time of irradiation. This disproves their initial hypothesis that space radiation-induced improvement in CDFC was reflective of increased hippocampal neurogenesis (or less of a decrease in hippocampal neurogenesis relative to controls) in mice that were mature at the time of irradiation. 
<p>
Aim 2: Determine how radiation influences the dendritic morphology of embryonic- versus adult- generated granule cell neurons. <br>
Investigators have collected multiple cohorts of Nestin-CreERT2/R26R-YFP mice that received Tamoxifen at postnatal day 3 and were irradiated in adulthood. These tissues are being currently examined for the effect of both 28Si and 56Fe on dendritic morphology in cells that were generated in the early postnatal period. 
<p>
Aim 3: Define the impact of radiation on the translational profile of genetically defined DG neural cells. <br>
Investigators have collected multiple cohorts of BAC-TRAP mice that were irradiated in adulthood. These tissues are being currently examined for the effect of both 28Si and 56Fe on RNAseq and microarray expression.
<p>
        </div>

